{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794768136,"nonce":"1YU1I31uUZ3Vvc7V03HH3/61Oh4=","title":"docs: statements that disagree with the code or with each other","message":"Found during a declauding pass over docs/, README.md and CONTRIBUTING.md. The prose pass left every one of these alone, because choosing which version is true belongs to the author. \"Verified\" means I checked the code or a source file myself; \"reported\" means a reviewing agent flagged it and I have not checked.\n\nVerified against the code\n- docs/debugging.md, the `ASH_AIR` row: \"v2 runs the interpreter over AIR instead of opcodes\" reads as opt-in. An unset `ASH_AIR` selects the SSA walker (crates/ash_interp/src/ssa.rs:58); `v2-serialize` is the flat-opcode arm; accepted values are v2, v2-serialize and off (air.rs:58-63).\n- docs/cli.md:55 says the default `application` preset compiles in Cranelift at 100 calls. The table in the same file says 10, and the code says 10 (jit) and 10,000 (opt) for that preset (crates/ash_interp/src/tiering.rs, default in crates/ash_cli/src/main.rs:231). README.md:81 says \"Cranelift compiles at 100 calls and LLVM recompiles at 1000\", which matches neither preset values nor TieredConfig::default() (100 / 250).\n- docs/wasm/README.md:8 describes wasm as \"AIR → LLVM IR → wasm32 object → link\", and line 123 mentions \"the LLVM patch it needs\". The direct backend is the only wasm backend (docs/aot.md's ASH_WASM_DIRECT row says so; commit 9b7d2d78 deleted the LLVM wasm code).\n- Broken cross-references from code to docs: std/src/gc.rs:5110 cites docs/wasm/README.md \"GC roots\", which does not exist; crates/ash_cli/src/main.rs:440 and crates/ash_cli/Cargo.toml:35 cite docs/windows-port.md, which is docs/internals/windows-port.md.\n- README.md \"Size\": \"(1.9 MB out of a 3.96 MB hello world)\". docs/wasm/hdlls.md and crates/ash_hdll_sqlite say the hello world went from 3.96 MB to 2.29 MB, a drop of 1.67 MB. 1.9 MB is the side module's own size.\n\nReported, not yet checked: the LLVM-for-wasm cluster\nThese look like leftovers from before the direct backend became the only one:\n- docs/aot.md: \"Persistent AIR reuse\" says LLVM IR emission, optimization, codegen and linking still run on each build for wasm; \"Optimization options\" applies -O, --codegen-opt-level and \"the faster WASM stackification path\" to wasm; \"Cross-compiling\" lists wasm32 among the LLVM backends and says cross_targets checks an ELF header.\n- docs/internals/wasm-exceptions.md cites the deleted crates/ash/src/llvm/function/wasm_traps.rs and describes LLVM invoke edges; the direct backend does this in crates/ash_wasm_emit/src/traps.rs with a try_table catching __c_longjmp.\n- docs/internals/wasm-abi.md cites the deleted crates/ash/src/llvm/aot_trampoline.rs; the signature keys are now in crates/ash/src/wasm_program.rs (signature_key).\n- docs/internals/wasm-fibers.md: three statements about LLVM's SjLj lowering and inlining may no longer describe the only wasm backend.\n\nReported, not yet checked: other disagreements\n- docs/cli.md: \"Executables link on the host only; a cross build stops at --emit-aot\" against its own \"Native cross builds\" section and docs/aot.md's Android and iOS section. The --opt-threshold table says \"interpreted calls only\"; the prose adds loop back-edges. \"This policy ends when the build finishes\" has no clear meaning.\n- docs/aot.md: \"8,577-function game\" against \"all 9,021 bodies\"; \"A non-host target is built for a generic CPU\" against cli.md's wording; the \"Limits of an AOT binary\" section ends on an aot_smoke test sentence that does not belong to it.\n- CONTRIBUTING.md: \"Haxe threads are krio fibers on one OS thread\" against docs/hdll.md and docs/internals/wasm-threads.md, which describe M:N.\n- docs/wasm/hdlls.md \"Not done\": \"The browser loader\" looks done (crates/ash_wasm_runtime/src/browser/dylink.rs); only lazy fetching is open.\n- README.md Conformance says eight cases need the `fmt` HDLL; docs/wasm/hdlls.md says `fmt` stays compiled into the runtime.\n- docs/internals/windows-port.md: SDL3.dll in one place and GetModuleHandleW(\"SDL2.dll\") in another; pump_events_and_swap and lower_own_priority were not found in the ash crates.\n- docs/internals/wasm-threads.md: \"Order of work if threads ship\" reads as a plan, while the same page says ash ships it and reports 3.01x.\n- docs/internals/wasm-abi.md says a function pointer is \"in the low hundreds\"; std/src/fiber.rs says \"below a couple of thousand\".\n- docs/internals/wasm-fibers.md Results gives +26% module size; the transform was last measured at +32% and +37%.\n- docs/debugging.md: \"the widener is the only O3 pass that rewrites arithmetic\" (tree fusion, FMA and the vectorizer also change arithmetic); the tree-fusion checkpoint sections say fusion is not enabled, and a later section says it is on by default.\n- docs/hdll.md says library `simd` never comes from an HDLL, then calls crates/ash_hdll_simd \"a complete HDLL in Rust\". docs/wasm/hdlls.md uses `wasm32-wasi` in one clang line and never names the third default export. docs/wasm/README.md says two hosts ship in crates/ash_wasm_runtime and lists crates/ash_browser as the second.\n- docs/wasm/*-build-times.md: issue ids appear as short ids (the full 64-character id avoids forge autolinking); llvm-build-times.md and air-cache-build-times.md report different wall and backend times for the same app and machine; shard-build-times.md does not say whether its baseline (a8f4140a) has the JIT promotion cap fix.\n\nNot covered by the prose pass: docs/aot.md and docs/hdll.md use AIR, HDLL, tiers and the platform table without defining them. They are written for people who already work on ash.","files":null}]}